title 01 | The complete journey: https://www.example.com/hello
participant "Client\nHTTP application" as C #DBEAFE
participant "DNS\nrecursive resolver" as D #DBEAFE
participant "Network path\nLAN -> gateway -> Internet" as N #FFEDD5
participant "Server\nHTTPS application" as S #DBEAFE

note over C,S
  READ FIRST: follow stages 1-8 downward; the other sources expand them.
  Example: HTTP/1.1 + TLS 1.3 + TCP + IPv4; a new connection.
  The addresses are illustrative, not the real DNS answer for this hostname.
  Client 192.168.1.10:51514; server 203.0.113.20:443.
end note

== 1 | Get a usable network [detail: 02] ==
C -> C: Link ready; IP address, subnet,\ndefault gateway, DNS resolver available
note right of C
  DHCP is optional setup when configuration is absent.
  Existing configuration skips this work.
end note

== 2 | Find the server address [detail: 02] ==
C -> D: DNS lookup: www.example.com\n(separate application exchange; if cache misses)
D --> C: A / AAAA answer + cache lifetime

== 3 | Open a reliable connection [detail: 03] ==
note over S #EDE9FE
  Server already has a listening socket on TCP port 443.
end note
C -> S: socket + connect; TCP SYN / SYN-ACK / ACK\nLogical exchange through the network path
S --> C: TCP connection established; server accepts socket

== 4 | Authenticate and secure the connection [detail: 03] ==
C -> S: TLS handshake over this TCP connection
S --> C: Server authenticated; traffic keys established
note over C,S #FCE7F3
  HTTP plaintext is visible at TLS endpoints.
  Transit devices normally forward encrypted payloads.
end note

== 5 | Send the request through every layer [detail: 04] ==
note over C #DBEAFE
  APPLICATION: GET /hello + Host header
  SECURITY: TLS encrypts into records
  SOCKET API: TLS writes bytes to the TCP socket
  TRANSPORT: TCP sends segments
  NETWORK: IP wraps segments into packets
  LINK: NIC builds local delivery frames
  PHYSICAL: NIC transmits encoded signals
end note
C -> N: Physical transmission on the first link
N -> N: Switch / AP: local frame forwarding\nRouters: IP forwarding + new outgoing link frames\nSignals are received and transmitted on each link
N -> S: Last link delivers signals to server NIC

== 6 | Receive and process [details: 03, 04] ==
note over S #DBEAFE
  PHYSICAL -> LINK -> IP -> TCP -> socket -> TLS -> HTTP
  Recover bits; validate frame; extract packet; reassemble bytes;
  verify/decrypt TLS; parse HTTP; call application handler.
end note
S -> S: Process /hello; prepare HTTP status, headers, body

== 7 | Send the response back [detail: 04] ==
S -> N: HTTP -> TLS -> socket -> TCP -> IP -> frame -> signals
N -> N: Forward toward client; reverse NAT if present\nThe return route can differ from the request route
N --> C: Signals -> frame -> IP -> TCP -> socket -> TLS -> HTTP
C -> C: Consume the response body\nA browser may now parse, render, and fetch more resources

== 8 | Reuse or close; handle delays and loss [detail: 05] ==
note over C,S #EDE9FE
  TCP ACKs and loss recovery run throughout data transfer.
  An ACK confirms transport receipt, not successful application processing.
  Reuse an established connection, or close TLS and then TCP gracefully.
end note

note over N
  [06] Alternatives: IPv6 / Neighbor Discovery; HTTP/3 / QUIC;
  CDN or reverse proxy termination with a separate origin connection.
  Every DNS, TCP, and TLS packet also uses network, link, and physical layers.
end note
legend left
  |= Color |= Role |
  |<#DBEAFE> Application | HTTP, DNS, application logic |
  |<#FCE7F3> Security | TLS above TCP; SSL is the historical name |
  |<#EDE9FE> Transport | TCP; sockets are a local API boundary |
  |<#FFEDD5> Network | IP addressing and router forwarding |
  |<#DCFCE7> Link | Ethernet / Wi-Fi framing and local delivery |
  |<#FEF3C7> Physical | Electrical, optical, or radio signals |
  Solid arrows: actions / exchanges. Dashed arrows: replies / completions.
  Arrow style does not imply a different delivery guarantee.
endlegend

