title 06 | Three independent variants of the overview journey
participant "Client HTTP / TLS\nor HTTP/3 / QUIC library" as C #DBEAFE
participant "Client OS\nsockets / IP / neighbors" as O #EDE9FE
participant "Local IPv6\nrouter" as G #FFEDD5
participant "Network path\nIP -> link -> physical" as N #FFEDD5
participant "CDN edge / proxy\nTLS + HTTP endpoint" as P #FCE7F3
participant "Server endpoint\ndirect server / origin" as S #DBEAFE

note over C,S
  READ FIRST: these panels are independent alternatives, not successive stages.
  A: IPv6 changes addressing and neighbor discovery.
  B: HTTP/3 changes transport to QUIC over UDP.
  C: Proxy termination changes where the client's connection ends.
  They can also be combined in a real deployment.
end note

== A | IPv6: change the network path; HTTP/TLS/TCP can remain ==
opt Initial IPv6 autoconfiguration is needed on this link
  O -> G: ICMPv6 Router Solicitation
  G --> O: Router Advertisement: prefix + router lifetime\nOther options as configured
  O -> O: Example SLAAC: form address from suitable prefix\nPerform Duplicate Address Detection as required
end
note over O,G #FFEDD5
  Example client address: 2001:db8:1::10; gateway link-local: fe80::1.
  SLAAC = stateless address autoconfiguration.
  A default router is learned from RA; DHCPv6 does not supply a default gateway.
  DNS configuration may come from RA options, DHCPv6, or other configuration.
end note
C -> O: Resolve www.example.com: request IPv6 candidates
O --> C: Illustrative AAAA answer: 2001:db8:2::20
C -> O: Select candidate; socket(AF_INET6, SOCK_STREAM); connect(...,443)
O -> O: IPv6 route lookup: choose next-hop router fe80::1
opt Next-hop neighbor mapping is absent
  O -> G: ICMPv6 Neighbor Solicitation\nTypically solicited-node multicast for address resolution
  G --> O: Neighbor Advertisement with link address
end
O -> N: TCP establishment + TLS + HTTP packets via IPv6\nEthernet / Wi-Fi frame -> physical signals
N -> S: Routers forward; decrease Hop Limit; replace link encapsulation
S --> N: Response IPv6 packets toward client
N --> O: Receive through physical -> link -> IPv6 -> TCP
O --> C: Socket -> TLS -> HTTP response
note over O,S #FFEDD5
  Neighbor Discovery uses ICMPv6, not ARP broadcast.
  Off-link packets use the router's link address, not a remote server's MAC.
  IPv6 has no base-header checksum; routers do not fragment packets.
  Packet too large: ICMPv6 Packet Too Big; sender adapts packet sizes.
  Dual-stack clients can race/stagger IPv6 and IPv4 candidates (Happy Eyeballs).
end note

== B | HTTP/3: HTTP semantics over QUIC, carried in UDP ==
C -> C: Discover HTTP/3 support, e.g. Alt-Svc / HTTPS DNS record\nChoose QUIC endpoint; fresh handshake in this example
C -> O: Create UDP socket; send QUIC datagrams\nUsually destination UDP port 443
note over C,O #EDE9FE
  HTTP/3 -> QUIC streams + integrated TLS handshake -> UDP -> IP -> link -> physical.
  A QUIC library commonly manages transport above a UDP socket.
  UDP provides datagrams; QUIC provides reliability, ordering within streams,
  congestion/flow control, packet protection, and connection IDs.
end note
C -> S: QUIC Initial packet: CRYPTO frames carry TLS ClientHello\nLogical exchange via UDP and all lower layers
S --> C: Initial: TLS ServerHello\nHandshake: encrypted TLS authentication messages + Finished
C -> C: Validate server identity; derive traffic secrets\nNegotiate ALPN h3
C -> S: Handshake packet carrying client Finished
C -> S: Protected 1-RTT packet(s): HTTP/3 HEADERS / DATA\non a QUIC request stream
S -> S: Parse HTTP/3; dispatch /hello handler
S --> C: Protected HTTP/3 response on request stream
note over C,S #FCE7F3
  QUIC carries TLS handshake messages in CRYPTO frames, not TLS records.
  Application data uses QUIC packet protection, not the TCP/TLS record path.
  Initial keys are publicly derivable: Initial protection is not secret authentication.
  QUIC ACKs acknowledge packets; lost stream data can be sent in new packets.
  Independent streams avoid TCP's cross-stream loss-induced ordering blockage;
  they still share connection resources and congestion effects.
  No TCP SYN handshake occurs. Resumed 0-RTT is optional and has replay constraints.
  QUIC CONNECTION_CLOSE ends the connection; there is no TCP FIN sequence.
end note
note over O,N #FFEDD5
  IPv4 or IPv6 still routes UDP packets, with hop-by-hop link/physical transmission.
  If HTTP/3 cannot connect and the service supports it, a client may try HTTP/2 or HTTP/1.1.
  Those attempts create TCP connections rather than turning QUIC into TCP.
end note

== C | CDN / reverse proxy: two separate connections and trust boundaries ==
C -> C: DNS / service routing selects a CDN edge or proxy address
C -> P: Connection A: TCP + TLS authenticated for www.example.com
C -> P: Encrypted HTTP request on connection A
P -> P: Terminate TLS; decrypt; apply HTTP routing / cache policy
alt CDN can serve the request from cache
  P --> C: Encrypt cached HTTP response on connection A
else Origin work is needed
  P -> S: Connection B: separate TCP + TLS to configured origin\nCreate or reuse origin connection; validate origin identity
  P -> S: Forward HTTP request protected by connection B
  S -> S: Handle request; create origin response
  S --> P: Response protected by connection B
  P -> P: Receive/decrypt origin response; apply response/cache policy
  P --> C: Encrypt client response with connection A's traffic keys
end
note over C,S #FCE7F3
  Connection A and B have independent sockets, keys, sequence spaces, and lifecycles.
  The proxy is a TLS endpoint and sees plaintext HTTP while processing it.
  The origin's transport peer is the proxy; client identity needs trusted metadata if required.
  Forwarded / X-Forwarded-* headers require an explicit trusted-proxy policy.
  Example B uses TLS; deployments can instead use plaintext or mutual TLS to the origin.
  TLS passthrough is another design: a proxy forwards bytes without terminating client TLS.
  A cache hit needs no origin request for this response.
end note
